"""One-shot build script: append one substantive paragraph to each note in batch-B.json
so every note body reaches the 400-900 word requirement. Run once, then validate."""
import json, os

adds = [
# ---------------- M09 ----------------
("IL29-M09", "The portal's mental model",
"""Here is the drill that makes inheritance concrete: pick any computer in your tree and trace its effective policy set by hand. Start at the organization level, list each policy attached there, then walk down through each group to the machine, noting where a lower level refines or overrides. Do this for three machines — one per client — and write down the results. Then compare against what the portal reports as effective policy. If they match, your mental model is correct; if not, you have found either a misunderstanding or a misconfiguration, and both are worth more than the ten minutes the drill took. Make this a quarterly exercise, and require it of every new admin before they touch a production policy. Inheritance you cannot trace by hand is inheritance you cannot troubleshoot at 2 AM."""),
("IL29-M09", "The admin's daily loop",
"""The daily loop has weekly and monthly siblings. Weekly: review the exception inventory for anything expiring, check version drift across the fleet, and read the week's Detect alerts as a set — patterns invisible day-to-day emerge weekly. Monthly: run the four compliance reports, review the change log for entries you cannot understand, and walk the group tree for cruft. The daily loop keeps you alive; the weekly and monthly loops keep the fleet healthy. Put all three on the calendar as recurring blocks, because loops that live only in your head die the first time you have a busy week — and busy weeks are when you need them most."""),
("IL29-M09", "Agent deployment states",
"""Turn health into a number you manage: define a fleet-hygiene target — for example 98 percent of agents healthy and current — and track it weekly on a simple trend line. When the line dips, investigate the cause class: is it one site (network or power), one machine type (imaging breaking updates), or random (user behavior)? Each class has a different fix, and the trend tells you which class you are fighting. Report the number monthly to whoever owns the risk, with every gap explained by name. A single tracked number, honestly reported, builds more trust than any dashboard — and it makes slow drift visible before it becomes a crisis."""),
("IL29-M09", "The policy lifecycle",
"""Version your policies like code: every change gets a version note — what changed and why — and the previous version stays restorable with one action. Never edit the production policy in place to try something; clone it, test the clone in the ring, then promote. Keep a staging scope — a group of test machines mirroring production's variety — permanently available, so there is always somewhere safe to try the risky change. The discipline feels slow until the day a bad edit takes thirty seconds to revert instead of three hours to reconstruct from memory. Policies are infrastructure; treat them with the change hygiene infrastructure deserves."""),
("IL29-M09", "Reports that carry",
"""Prepare for the questions auditors actually ask, because they are sharper than 'are you protected?' They ask: show me a denied execution from last month and walk me through what happened next. Who approved this exception, and where is the business justification? Your change log shows a policy edit at 2 AM — who authorized it? Two agents are unhealthy — why, and what is the plan? Rehearse these with your own reports before the auditor arrives: pull one denied execution and narrate the triage, open the exception inventory and read the justifications aloud, account for every unhealthy agent by name. The audit is a performance, and the rehearsal is what makes it boring — boring audits are the ones you pass."""),
("IL29-M09", "Designing organization structure",
"""Design for the acquisition you have not had yet. Sooner or later a client acquires a company, or your MSP onboards a client with an existing mess, and your clean tree meets reality. The pattern: create the new organization or group branch as a quarantine zone with a restrictive baseline policy, run Learning Mode baselining on its machines, and migrate them into your standard structure group by group — never by dumping them into production groups on day one. The quarantine zone is the ring structure applied to mergers: observe, categorize, then integrate. Document the migration the same way you document a rollout, because in six months nobody will remember which machines came from the acquisition — except the tree, if you built it right."""),
("IL29-M09", "Operator gotchas",
"""Protect against your own absence: the bus factor. If you were unavailable tomorrow, could your successor run the fleet from your documentation alone? Test it: hand your design document, runbooks, and change log to a peer and ask them to perform the daily loop and trace three machines' effective policies. Every question they ask is a documentation gap; fix them. Keep credentials, vendor contacts, and escalation paths in the team vault, not your head. The ultimate operator gotcha is not a technical mistake — it is being the single point of failure for the entire security posture. Heroes document themselves into replaceability; that is what makes the fleet survive them."""),
# ---------------- M10 ----------------
("IL29-M10", "What 'hero' support",
"""Measure what matters and distrust what is easy to game. Response time is easy to measure and easy to game — a stream of 'looking into it!' updates proves nothing. Track instead: reopen rate (did the fix stick?), time-to-correct-diagnosis (when did you actually know the cause?), and customer effort (how many times did they repeat themselves?). A hero with a twelve-minute response and a tiny reopen rate beats a two-minute responder with chronic reopen churn on every metric the business actually feels. Review these monthly as a team, and celebrate the low-reopen techs publicly — what you celebrate is what the team optimizes for."""),
("IL29-M10", "Ticket intake",
"""The vague ticket — 'internet is slow,' 'computer is acting weird' — needs a funnel, not a guess. Start wide: when did you first notice it, and is it constant or intermittent? Then narrow: is it one application or everything, one machine or several, wired or wireless? Then test: can you try this specific action right now and tell me exactly what happens? Each answer eliminates a branch; three questions usually isolate it to an app, a machine, or the network. Write the funnel answers into the intake as you go — 'slow internet' becomes 'intermittent latency on wireless, all apps, two laptops in Building B, started Tuesday after the access-point firmware update.' The vague ticket was never vague; it was just unasked."""),
("IL29-M10", "Severity classification",
"""Severity disputes are inevitable: the customer insists their SEV-4 is a SEV-1, or your manager thinks your SEV-2 is really a SEV-3. Handle both with the matrix, not authority. To the customer: acknowledge the impact, state your committed response plan, explain the classification in impact facts, and name the trigger that would reclassify upward immediately. To the manager: present the impact facts and the matrix, not your feelings. The matrix is the shared language that depersonalizes the disagreement. And when you are wrong — the SEV-4 that becomes a SEV-1 — say so plainly and reclassify fast; defending a wrong call to save face is how small incidents become big ones."""),
("IL29-M10", "Escalation paths",
"""Escalate sideways before you escalate up. The peer who fixed this exact issue last month is faster than the senior tier's queue — check the ticket history for similar symptoms and reach the resolving tech directly with your context package. Keep a team channel where stuck techs post the five-element package; make it culturally safe to ask early by celebrating the quick ask, not the lone-wolf grind. Upward escalation is for authority and capacity; sideways escalation is for knowledge. Most stuck tickets need knowledge, not rank — and the team that shares freely resolves faster than the team of brilliant silos."""),
("IL29-M10", "Communication under pressure",
"""The incident ends, but the communication is not over. Send the follow-up within 24 hours: what happened in one paragraph with no blame, what you fixed, what you changed so it does not recur, and an invitation for feedback. For SEV-1s, add the timeline: detected at, acknowledged at, mitigated at, resolved at. This message does three jobs: it closes the emotional loop for the customer, it creates the written record the post-mortem needs, and it demonstrates the systemic improvement that justifies their trust. Customers forgive outages; they do not forgive being left wondering. The follow-up is where a bad day becomes a trust-building story."""),
("IL29-M10", "Documentation discipline",
"""Turn ticket notes into a knowledge base, or the knowledge dies in the ticket archive. Monthly, harvest the best-resolved tickets: extract the symptom-diagnosis-fix triple into a searchable article, tag it by application and error, and link the source tickets. When the same issue recurs, the next tech finds the article in seconds instead of rediscovering the diagnosis. Assign article ownership — someone keeps each one current as software updates. A support team with a living knowledge base gets faster every quarter; a team without one solves the same problems forever. The documentation discipline from this module compounds only if you build the harvesting habit on top of it."""),
("IL29-M10", "Talking about support",
"""Prepare questions for them, because the panel scores curiosity too. Ask: what does the escalation path look like from this role — when would I pull in engineering? How does the team balance fast approvals against careful verification? What is the most common breakage pattern new hires struggle with? Each question shows you already think like an operator: you are asking about the real job, not the perks. Avoid questions the job posting already answers, and never ask anything suggesting you skipped basic research. End with: what would make someone exceptional in this role in the first 90 days? Then listen carefully, because they just handed you the grading rubric."""),
# ---------------- M11 ----------------
("IL29-M11", "The diagnosis workflow",
"""Time-box each step so diagnosis does not become archaeology. Identification: fifteen minutes — if you cannot name the blocked action from the events in that time, escalate with what you have. Policy check: fifteen minutes — read the denying rule and judge its intent. Exception and test: the canary test starts within the hour for SEV-2 and above. Documentation: written before you close the ticket, no exceptions. The time-boxes are not about rushing; they are about recognizing when you are stuck. A tech who escalates at minute thirty with clean notes outperforms the hero who emerges at hour three with a fix and no trail — because the next identical ticket takes the first tech ten minutes and the second tech another three hours."""),
("IL29-M11", "Reading block events",
"""Correlate across machines: a single block event is a ticket, but the same block on twenty machines is an incident with a different response. When you see a deny, ask 'who else?' — query the event feed for the same process, parent, or target across the fleet in the same window. The payroll-helper block on six machines meant a vendor update, not six individual problems; the fix was one policy change, not six tickets. Fleet-wide correlation also catches the attack: the same malicious pattern on five machines is a campaign, and your response shifts from break-fix to incident response. Train yourself to zoom out before you zoom in — the pattern across machines usually reveals the cause faster than any single event's details."""),
("IL29-M11", "Breakage pattern: updaters",
"""Make the managed-versus-unmanaged decision explicitly for every application class. Managed updates — pushed through your deployment tool on your schedule — give you testing, timing control, and known identities; they are the right answer for line-of-business software where a surprise break costs real money. Auto-updates are acceptable for low-risk, frequently-patched software where the security cost of delay exceeds the breakage risk — but then your policy must approve the updater's behavior, not chase hashes. Unmanaged per-user installs are the enemy of both: no testing, no timing, no inventory. Write the decision down per application class, because 'we will figure out updates later' is how Monday mornings get ruined."""),
("IL29-M11", "Breakage pattern: macros",
"""The sustainable answer for macro-heavy businesses is trusted locations plus signing, not per-macro exceptions forever. Designate controlled shares where macro-enabled files live; the policy permits macro execution only for files in those locations, and only those locations are writable by the document owners — not by email attachments or downloads. Push the business toward signing macros: a signed macro from a known publisher is verifiable in a way an unsigned one never is. The migration path: inventory current macros, move them to trusted locations, sign what matters, and set a date after which unsigned macros outside trusted locations stop working. It is a project, not a ticket — but it ends the endless exception treadmill for macros."""),
("IL29-M11", "The exception discipline",
"""Run the exception review as a real meeting, quarterly, with the owners in the room. The agenda is mechanical: for each active exception, the owner answers 'do you still need this?' in thirty seconds. Still needed: confirm the scope is still minimal and reset the review date. Not needed: remove it now, verify nothing breaks, document the removal. Owner absent: default to removal after one grace cycle — exceptions without advocates are the ones that rot. The meeting takes an hour for a healthy inventory and pays for itself the first time it kills a years-old exception nobody remembered. Publish the minutes: the inventory, the removals, the renewals. Visibility keeps exceptions deliberate instead of folkloric."""),
("IL29-M11", "Testing exceptions",
"""Choose canaries for representativeness, not convenience. Cover the axes of variation: machine types (laptop versus desktop versus kiosk), OS and driver versions (the driver-variant miss from the deployment module is the cautionary tale), software combinations (the machine with the weird legacy app), and user behaviors (the power user who exercises edge features). Five machines spanning the variation beat twenty identical ones. Recruit volunteers explicitly — they get fixes first and a direct line to support — and rotate them so nobody burns out. Document the canary set per application class, because the right canaries for the billing app differ from those for the warehouse scanners. Representativeness is the whole game."""),
("IL29-M11", "When it is not the policy",
"""Build the 'not the policy' evidence package as a habit, because you will present it weekly. The package: the block-event query for the failure window showing zero denies for the failing action, the reproduction on a machine outside the policy scope failing identically, and your alternate hypothesis with its own evidence — the vendor's update notes, the application's error log. Present it as help, not defense: 'I have ruled out the policy with these two tests — here is what I found instead, and here is my recommended next step.' Users who see this package twice stop blaming the platform reflexively; they start bringing you better symptoms. The evidence package does not just win the argument — it trains the organization to troubleshoot with you instead of against you."""),
("IL29-M11", "Change control for people",
"""Audit the change log monthly, because the process rots silently. Read every entry and ask: do I understand what changed and why? Is the approver named? Is there an undo path? Flag the entries that fail — not to punish, but to find where the process is too heavy (people bypass heavy processes) or too vague (the template needs better prompts). Track two numbers: what fraction of policy changes have complete log entries, and how many emergency changes got their within-the-hour documentation. A process with total bypass is not a process; lighten it until compliance is the path of least resistance. Change control that people actually follow beats perfect change control that everyone routes around."""),
# ---------------- M12 ----------------
("IL29-M12", "Rollout phases",
"""Run servers on a separate, slower track — never in the user rings. Servers have different risk profiles (availability is critical, change windows are narrow), different software (fewer apps, each one load-bearing), and different blast radius (one bad policy halts the business, not just one user). The server track mirrors the ring shape — lab, one canary server, then the fleet — but on maintenance-window scheduling with explicit rollback rehearsals. Harden server policies beyond the workstation baseline: servers run fewer applications, so their allowlists can be shorter and their Ringfencing stricter. The most expensive deployment failures are server policies pushed with workstation assumptions; the separate track makes that mistake structurally impossible."""),
("IL29-M12", "Learning Mode baselining",
"""Run the baseline review as a working meeting, not a solo task. Attendees: you, one person from IT who knows the software inventory, and a representative from each major department. Walk the categorized list — approve, scope, updater-rule, reject, investigate — making decisions out loud and recording each with its rationale. The department reps catch what IT misses ('we still use that old label tool twice a year'), and their presence builds the buy-in you will need at enforcement. Time-box the investigate items: each gets an owner and a 48-hour deadline, not an indefinite parking lot. End with the approved baseline signed off — that signature turns 'IT's project' into 'our policy.'"""),
("IL29-M12", "Stakeholder communication",
"""If leadership will not sponsor visibly, do not launch — a rollout without air cover fails at the first executive escalation, and you will own the political damage. When the one-pager comes back unsigned, diagnose the objection: cost, disruption fear, or disbelief in the threat? Answer with the matching evidence — pilot block data for disbelief, the ring structure and rollback plan for disruption fear, incident-cost math for cost objections. Offer a smaller commitment: sponsor just the pilot, with a go/no-go they control. Most resistance melts after Ring 1 produces real numbers. But if sponsorship is truly absent, say so plainly and pause — launching deny-by-default into an unsupportive executive team is a career-limiting move dressed as initiative."""),
("IL29-M12", "Go/no-go gates",
"""Document every gate decision with its data, and write the no-go recovery plan before you need it. The gate record: the four metrics, the decision (go, conditional go, no-go), any conditions, and the signatures. The no-go plan, written in advance: what gets fixed, who owns each fix, the re-gate date, and what the held rings do meanwhile. A no-go without a recovery plan becomes an indefinite stall — the project loses momentum and dies quietly. A no-go with a dated recovery plan is just a delay with a purpose. Teams that handle their first no-go well earn the credibility that makes every subsequent gate honest; teams that never say no-go eventually ship a failure."""),
("IL29-M12", "Rollback plans",
"""Rehearse the rollback before Ring 1 — a game day, not a document review. Pick a test group, push a deliberately breaking policy change, and execute the Tier 1 revert under time pressure: who authorizes, what gets clicked, how you verify service restored, how you communicate. Time it. The rehearsal always reveals something: the revert needs a permission nobody has, the verification step is vague, the communication template is missing. Fix all of it now, while the stakes are zero, and write the lessons into the runbook. The team that has rolled back on purpose rolls back calmly in the incident; the team meeting the revert button for the first time at 2 AM does not."""),
("IL29-M12", "Success metrics",
"""Build the dashboard in a spreadsheet before you automate anything — the manual version teaches you which numbers actually matter. One tab per metric, one row per week: coverage with gap explanations, block volume by category, exception inventory with review dates, tickets per week with the ring annotated, time-to-approve median. Add a notes column for context ('Ring 2 wave 1 started,' 'holiday week'). Review it at every gate and monthly in steady state. Only automate after three months of manual tracking, when you know exactly which numbers you read and which you ignore. The spreadsheet-first discipline prevents the classic failure: an expensive automated dashboard of metrics nobody reads, while the number that matters lives in someone's head."""),
("IL29-M12", "The 90-day plan",
"""Run the retrospective with the whole project team within two weeks of day 90, while memories are fresh. The format is fixed: what worked (keep doing), what broke (fix the process), what surprised us (update the risk-register template for next time), and what we would tell the next team (the handover wisdom). Write it down — the retrospective document is the most valuable artifact of the project for the organization, more than the plan itself, because the next deployment starts from it instead of from zero. Include the numbers: final metrics, total tickets, total exceptions, hours spent. End with the ownership transfer: who owns the platform now, what their first 30 days look like, and when you check back. Projects end; operations continue — the retrospective is the bridge."""),
# ---------------- M13 ----------------
("IL29-M13", "What a threat model is",
"""When everything feels critical, rank assets by asking two questions: what does losing it cost (money, legal exposure, operational halt), and what does protecting it cost? Assets where loss-cost dwarfs protection-cost are the priority; assets where protection costs more than the loss are candidates for acceptance — and acceptance is a documented decision with an owner, not an oversight. Revisit the ranking when the business changes, because asset value moves: the archive nobody cared about becomes critical during litigation, the dev server becomes critical when it holds the only copy of the new product. Ranking is not a one-time sorting; it is a living judgment about what the business cannot afford to lose."""),
("IL29-M13", "First-principles reasoning",
"""Apply the loop to purchasing decisions to kill shelfware. Goal: state precisely what the tool must achieve against which adversary — 'detect ransomware-affiliate tooling on workstations within minutes' is a goal; 'improve security' is not. Adversary: name the tier from this module's taxonomy. Mechanism: how does the tool actually achieve the goal — technically, not per the datasheet? Iterate: attack the claim — what does it miss, what are its blind spots, what does it assume? Then compare against not buying it: can existing controls, configured better, achieve the goal? Half of security purchases fail this loop, which is exactly why running it saves money. Bring the one-page loop analysis to every procurement discussion; it ends vague enthusiasm with specific questions."""),
("IL29-M13", "Adversary capability levels",
"""Stay current without drowning: build a threat-intel diet with three tiers. Daily skim: one reputable security news source, headlines only, five minutes — watching for your industry and your stack. Weekly read: one deep analysis of a campaign or technique relevant to your adversary tiers — this is where you learn the ransomware affiliate's current tooling. Quarterly review: revisit your adversary list itself — has the business changed, have you become interesting to a new tier? Unsubscribe from everything else; intel you do not read is noise that trains you to ignore intel. And always translate: every item should end with 'so we should...' — a control to check, a detection to verify, or a conscious 'not our tier.'"""),
("IL29-M13", "Attack surface: counting",
"""Enumerate the shadow-IT surface: the SaaS apps nobody approved, the personal cloud storage syncing company data, the departmental database running on someone's desktop. Find them by asking the network (DNS logs reveal the SaaS domains), the endpoints (installed-software inventory), and finance (the credit-card subscriptions). Each discovery gets the same three questions: must it exist, must it be reachable, is it least-privilege? Most shadow IT exists for a legitimate reason the official process failed to serve — so the fix is often approving and governing it, not just banning it. The surface you do not know about is the surface you cannot defend; the enumeration is uncomfortable precisely because it works."""),
("IL29-M13", "Trust boundaries and where",
"""Treat every internal API as a trust-boundary crossing, because it is one. The mobile app calling the backend, the web frontend calling the microservice, the scheduled job calling the database — each crosses from one trust zone to another, and each needs its guard: authentication (who is calling), authorization (may they do this), and validation (is the input sane). The 'it is internal' exemption is how breaches propagate: the compromised web tier calls the internal API with no authentication, and the attacker rides it straight to the data. When you review an architecture, draw every API call as a boundary crossing and demand its guard by name. Unguarded internal APIs are the trust-boundary failure you will find most often."""),
("IL29-M13", "Worked example: threat-model",
"""Run the file-server model as a tabletop drill with the team: one person plays each adversary tier and narrates their attack path step by step while the defenders check whether the mitigations actually stop it. The ransomware affiliate's player will find the gaps the document missed — 'your backups are on the same network segment, so I encrypt those too.' The insider's player will find the permission the audit overlooked. The drill takes ninety minutes and produces more improvements than a month of document reviews, because adversarial thinking is a skill exercised, not a document written. Record the findings, update the model, and schedule the next drill after the next architecture change. The model lives in the drill, not on the page."""),
("IL29-M13", "Threat-modeling an endpoint fleet",
"""Build the asset-class matrix as the fleet's standing reference: rows are asset classes (finance workstations, executive laptops, servers, kiosks), columns are the model elements (ranked assets, primary adversary tier, key surface entries, trust boundaries, assigned controls). Fill it once, review it quarterly. New systems get classified into the matrix before deployment — 'which row is this?' becomes the intake question for every new project. The matrix turns threat modeling from a per-project effort into organizational muscle memory: the reasoning is done once per class, then applied by reference. When the panel asks how you would secure their fleet, sketching this matrix from memory shows you operate at scale."""),
("IL29-M13", "Common mistakes: threat-model theater",
"""Invite attack on your own models: the red-team review. Hand your threat model to a sharp colleague and ask them to break it — find the missing adversary, the unguarded crossing, the mitigation that does not actually mitigate. Reward the findings publicly; the reviewer who finds the hole you missed just saved you from the incident that would have found it. Do this before every major deployment, and rotate reviewers so fresh eyes see it each time. A model that survives a hostile review is worth ten that survived only their author's optimism. This is the iterate step made social — and the fastest way to build a team that thinks adversarially by habit."""),
# ---------------- M14 ----------------
("IL29-M14", "How programs use the stack",
"""See it in a debugger to make it concrete: break at a function's entry and inspect the frame — the saved registers, the locals' addresses, and the return address sitting at a specific stack location. Dump the raw stack words and watch the return address as just another value among the data. This is not exploit development; it is literacy — the operator who has seen the return address as a memory value understands viscerally why an overflow reaches it. Spend thirty minutes with a debugger and a toy program; the abstract diagram becomes a physical place, and every defense in this module becomes obviously necessary rather than theoretically nice."""),
("IL29-M14", "What a buffer overflow is",
"""Meet the overflow's cousin: the integer overflow, where arithmetic wraps around instead of spilling. A program computes a buffer size from user input; with a huge input the multiplication wraps to a small number, the program allocates the small buffer, then copies the full amount into it — an overflow born from arithmetic, not from a long string. The lesson generalizes: memory unsafety is a family — overflows, integer wraps, use-after-free, format-string bugs — all violating the programmer's mental model of memory. Defenses must therefore be systemic (canaries, DEP, ASLR, safe languages, sandboxing) rather than bug-specific. When you hear of a new memory-bug class, ask which defense layer catches it — the answer tells you whether your architecture is deep or merely decorated."""),
("IL29-M14", "From overflow to control hijack",
"""Understand return-oriented programming conceptually, because it is the adaptation that defines modern exploitation. DEP killed injected code, so attackers stopped injecting: instead they overwrite the return address to point at tiny snippets of the program's own code — each ending in a return instruction — chaining them so each snippet's return lands on the next. Like words forming a sentence, the snippets compose attacker behavior from the program's own vocabulary. The defense implications: DEP alone is insufficient, ASLR matters enormously (the attacker must know where the snippets live), and verifying that returns go to legitimate callers becomes the next frontier. Defenses do not end exploitation; they escalate it, and each escalation demands the next layer."""),
("IL29-M14", "Defense: stack canaries",
"""Notice the canary's clever detail: canary values typically include null bytes, and that is not an accident. Many overflow vectors are string operations that stop at null bytes — so a canary containing nulls is harder to overwrite precisely without terminating the copy early, and harder to leak via string disclosure. This is defense design at its finest: the mechanism exploits the attacker's own tools' limitations. The broader lesson: good defenses are not just walls but traps shaped by attacker constraints. When you evaluate any mitigation, ask what attacker capability it specifically taxes — the canary taxes precise memory writes, DEP taxes code injection, ASLR taxes address knowledge. Each tax compounds; the attacker's budget is finite."""),
("IL29-M14", "Defense: DEP/NX",
"""Grasp the entropy intuition: on 64-bit systems the address space is astronomically larger than on 32-bit, so ASLR has vastly more room to randomize — guessing a randomized address goes from feasible-with-patience to effectively impossible. This is why the 64-bit transition was a bigger security upgrade than most realize: it did not just add memory, it multiplied attacker uncertainty. The lesson for operators: architecture choices — 64-bit everywhere, strong ASLR defaults verified with your own checks from the lab — are security decisions, not just performance ones. When you standardize the fleet, standardize the strong defaults. Entropy is a resource, and you want the maximum your platform offers."""),
("IL29-M14", "Why allowlisting changes",
"""Value the detection bonus: every denied exploit attempt is a free indicator of compromise. The hijacked viewer denied at the script-interpreter launch tells you which machine, which document, which technique — intelligence you would never get from a silent successful compromise. Feed these denies into the detection workflow: the single attempt gets investigated via the incident-response path, the pattern across machines triggers the campaign response. This inverts the usual economics: the exploitation attempt becomes your sensor. Brief the team accordingly — 'blocked' does not mean 'ignore'; it means an adversary just showed their hand."""),
("IL29-M14", "Common misconceptions and the road",
"""Follow the supply chain: your software's memory bugs are often your dependencies' memory bugs. The document library, the image codec, the network stack — written in memory-unsafe languages, maintained by someone else, updated on their schedule. Your threat model must therefore include the dependency's patch cadence: a critical overflow in a library you embed is your vulnerability until you ship their fix. Maintain a software bill of materials for critical systems, subscribe to the dependencies' security advisories, and test their updates in your canary ring like any other change. The modern exploit often enters through the library you forgot you shipped — supply-chain hygiene is memory-safety hygiene."""),
# ---------------- M15 ----------------
("IL29-M15", "Least privilege in software",
"""Apply least privilege to secrets and credentials, because credentials are authority in concentrated form. Audit where your systems store them: the config file with the database password readable by every developer, the API key baked into the mobile app, the service account whose password has not rotated in years. Each is ambient authority waiting to be stolen — and stolen credentials bypass every privilege-separation design downstream. The fixes are the same principles: scope secrets to the component that needs them, rotate them on a schedule, and prefer short-lived issued credentials over long-lived stored ones. A beautiful privilege-separated architecture with a hardcoded admin password is a vault with the combination written on the door."""),
("IL29-M15", "Privilege separation: splitting",
"""Recognize the pattern in the wild: modern browsers separate into a privileged browser process and unprivileged renderer processes, one per tab — the renderer parses the hostile web with no privilege and asks the browser process for the few operations it needs through a validated channel. A renderer exploit yields a useless unprivileged process; the attacker must separately defeat the browser process to escape. This is this module's design running on billions of devices — proof that privilege separation scales to the most attacked software on earth. When you advocate for separation in your own designs, point at the browser: the pattern is not academic, it is why web exploitation is hard instead of trivial."""),
("IL29-M15", "Capabilities as unforgeable",
"""Know the lineage briefly: capability thinking comes from operating-system research stretching back decades — systems built around unforgeable tokens rather than user identities, where every access names its capability explicitly. The ideas survived because the problems they solve never went away: ambient authority keeps producing confused deputies in every generation of software. You do not need the history's details, but knowing the lineage helps you recognize capability-shaped solutions in modern systems — file descriptors, scoped API tokens, per-app mobile permissions — and advocate for them with the confidence that the concept has been battle-tested far beyond any single product."""),
("IL29-M15", "Ambient authority and the confused",
"""Spot the web's confused deputy: cross-site request forgery. Your browser holds your ambient authority as a logged-in session, and a malicious site tricks your browser into wielding it — submitting a transfer, changing your settings — while you merely visited a page. The browser was the confused deputy, unable to distinguish your intent from the attacker's. The fixes are capability-shaped: per-session anti-forgery tokens bind the authority to the legitimate page, and cookie policies stop the ambient authority from traveling cross-site. Every time you implement or review these defenses, you are applying this module's lesson — the deputy problem is not a historical curiosity but a daily web reality."""),
("IL29-M15", "How this shows up in Elevation",
"""Design the approval workflow with two paths: the fast path for known-good patterns (pre-approved application identities elevate in seconds, logged for audit) and the deliberate path for the novel (unknown requests get human review with the business justification). The fast path keeps the business moving — this is what prevents the 'give me admin back' revolt. The deliberate path is where judgment lives — and where you collect the data to promote patterns into the fast path over time. Measure both paths' latency like the deployment module's time-to-approve metric, because the workflow's speed is the architecture's political survival. A just-in-time system with multi-hour latency is just-in-time in name only."""),
("IL29-M15", "How this shows up in Ringfencing",
"""Think of per-application Ringfencing as a firewall where the 'network' is the operating system: just as a network firewall defines which hosts may talk to which, the application policy defines which resources each application may touch. The mental model transfers directly — default deny, explicit allows, rules scoped to identity (the application, not the address), and denied attempts logged as intelligence. When you explain Ringfencing to network engineers, use their vocabulary: it is a host firewall for application behavior. The analogy is honest because the principles are identical; only the enforcement point moved. And like firewall rules, application rules need the same hygiene: reviewed, minimal, documented, with expiry on the temporary."""),
("IL29-M15", "Design exercise: privilege-separating",
"""Practice the whiteboard narrative until it is smooth, because the panel will ask for exactly this shape: 'Design something secure.' Open with the threat model (thirty seconds), sketch the split (one minute), define the narrow channel and its validation (one minute), attack your own design out loud (one minute), and close with honest residuals (thirty seconds). The self-attack is the differentiator — most candidates present a design; few volunteer its weaknesses before being asked. Bring a marker-friendly diagram: two boxes, one arrow, the validation checks labeled on the arrow. Five minutes, one whiteboard, zero bluffing — that is the performance this module has been building toward."""),
# ---------------- M16 ----------------
("IL29-M16", "What sandboxing is",
"""Look at your phone: mobile platforms sandbox every app by default — each app gets its own storage, must request permission for the camera or contacts, and cannot read other apps' data. That is sandboxing as consumer infrastructure on billions of devices, and it is why phone malware is rarer and less damaging than desktop malware was at the same age. The desktop endpoint is catching up through application sandboxing. When users ask why their desktop app 'cannot just' do something, the phone analogy lands instantly: your phone's apps work the same way — they ask permission because the alternative is any app reading everything. Familiar examples make the abstract policy concrete."""),
("IL29-M16", "Sandboxing native code",
"""Confront the just-in-time compilation problem to appreciate the difficulty: JIT compilers — in browsers, in runtimes — legitimately need to write machine code and then execute it, which is exactly what write-xor-execute forbids. The solutions are intricate dances: separating the writing from the executing across processes, or toggling permissions in narrow windows — each dance a potential vulnerability. This is why browser JITs are among the most attacked components on earth: they live at the exact tension point of the sandbox model. The lesson: wherever legitimate functionality conflicts with the security invariant, the engineering gets subtle and the bugs get valuable. Identify those tension points in your own systems — they are where the next escape will come from."""),
("IL29-M16", "seccomp and syscall",
"""Study the progressive pattern used by well-engineered network services: the network-facing code runs unprivileged and, after the dangerous phases complete, sandboxes itself further — shedding even the system calls it no longer needs. The principle generalizes beautifully: sandboxing can tighten over a program's lifetime, from parsing untrusted input (needs file reads) to serving validated requests (needs almost nothing). When you design sandboxes, ask whether the policy can shrink as the program progresses — the parser needs file reads during startup but not after. Progressive tightening means a late-stage compromise inherits an even smaller box. The best sandbox is the one that keeps getting smaller."""),
("IL29-M16", "The isolation spectrum",
"""Know the middle ground: microVMs — virtual machines stripped to the minimum, booting in milliseconds with tiny memory footprints — built for the 'VM isolation at container speed' use case. They do not replace the spectrum's reasoning; they add a point on it, letting you buy near-VM isolation for workloads that could never afford full VMs. The operator lesson is not the technology but the habit: revisit your isolation choices as the mechanisms evolve, because a choice that was correct three years ago may be wrong today. The spectrum moves; your architecture should move with it, guided always by the threat model rather than by novelty."""),
("IL29-M16", "The performance-security tradeoff",
"""Use the latency-budget method for services: start from the user-visible budget — say two hundred milliseconds per request — allocate it across components, and give security mechanisms their explicit slice. If the sandbox costs two milliseconds of a two-hundred-millisecond budget, the discussion is over — it fits. If it costs fifty, you negotiate: can the check be cached, moved off the hot path, or applied selectively to untrusted inputs? The budget turns 'too slow' from a veto into an engineering problem with numbers. And it exposes the real tradeoffs: teams routinely spend large slices on analytics tracking while vetoing slivers of sandboxing — the budget makes the priorities visible, which is often the actual problem."""),
("IL29-M16", "Mapping sandboxing onto Ringfencing",
"""Review policies with a checklist, quarterly per application: is the identity still correct (no renames, no updater drift)? Is every allow still in the job description (prune the cruft)? Are the denies still holding (spot-check the block logs for the actions that should be denied)? Has the application gained features needing new analysis? Is there a temporary loosening past its expiry? The checklist takes twenty minutes per app and prevents the two slow killers: policy drift (accumulating cruft) and bit rot (rules that stopped matching reality). Assign each application's review to a named owner — policies without owners are policies without maintenance, and unmaintained sandboxes are decorative."""),
("IL29-M16", "When sandboxes fail",
"""Think like the escape researcher for a day: their workflow is recon (map the sandbox's boundary — which syscalls, which IPC channels, which helpers), differential analysis (what does the sandboxed app do differently — the differences are the policy), and chaining (the parser bug gets code execution, the helper's permissive channel gets the escape — two medium bugs become one critical). Borrow the workflow defensively: recon your own sandbox's boundary, enumerate its helpers and their authority, and ask which two-bug chain breaks it. Researchers publish their escapes; read them as free audits of the patterns you deploy. Every public sandbox escape is a lesson someone else paid for — collect them."""),
]

PATH = "/home/hatch/workspace/apex-audit/docs/university/data/courses/_il29-build/batch-B.json"

def main():
    with open(PATH) as f:
        d = json.load(f)
    matched = 0
    for mid, tstart, para in adds:
        mod = next(x for x in d if x["id"] == mid)
        note = next(x for x in mod["notes"] if x["title"].startswith(tstart))
        note["body"] = note["body"] + "\n\n" + para
        matched += 1
    print("appended paragraphs:", matched)
    # validate word counts
    bad = []
    for m in d:
        for n in m["notes"]:
            wc = len(n["body"].split())
            if not (400 <= wc <= 900):
                bad.append((m["id"], n["title"][:40], wc))
    if bad:
        print("STILL OUT OF RANGE:")
        for b in bad:
            print(" ", b)
    else:
        print("all note bodies within 400-900 words")
    # atomic write
    tmp = PATH + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(d, f, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)
        f.write("\n")
    os.replace(tmp, PATH)
    print("wrote", PATH)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
